WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
I am taking this month off, and while I'm gone,

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:06.279
five other YouTube creators have lined up
some brilliant guest videos for you.

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:08.520
We start with science presenter, Steve Mould.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.400
And when he told me the title of this video
I thought,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.070
“No, I can't.”

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:14.630
And then I was wrong.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:18.600
So: be nice, be kind; Steve, take it away!

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.760
You have a super power that you’re probably
not aware of,

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.010
the ability to hear temperature, or at least,

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.949
the ability to discern between hot and cold
water just using your sense of hearing

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:31.550
and this is something we can test.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.679
So, I’m going to pour hot and cold water
from these identical jugs

00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:38.409
into these identical mugs

00:00:38.409 --> 00:00:41.219
and it’s your job to figure out which is
which.

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:42.940
And I’m going to blur the screen as well

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.059
because there may be some visual cues that
would tip you off,

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:51.489
one way or the other.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:58.409
Okay, here we go.

00:00:58.409 --> 00:01:00.660
It should have been quite easy to tell which
was which.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.999
Feel free to pause the video and leave a comment
with your guess, if you like,

00:01:03.999 --> 00:01:08.540
but here’s the answer: the first pour was
cold and the second pour was hot.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.280
And the reason they sound different is because
of the fact

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.250
that the water is viscous -- or thick, if
you like,

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:20.159
and the viscosity of water goes down when
you heat it.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:23.450
This is something that’s easier to see with
a liquid like honey.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.229
So, warm honey is more runny than cold honey,

00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:31.439
whereas, with water, it’s less visually
apparent.

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:33.359
But when you pour water,

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:38.970
the way it splashes into a cup is affected
by the viscosity.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:45.650
And this change in the splashing of the water
changes the sound that it makes

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:53.259
because of various complex fluid dynamic reasons.

00:01:53.259 --> 00:02:02.469
What’s really interesting is this is knowledge
you already had

00:02:02.469 --> 00:02:08.229
due to a lifetime of hearing cold drinks and
hot drinks being made.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:13.590
And like many things, this is something that
you knew intuitively,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:17.690
even though it wasn’t something you were
aware of consciously.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.050
Go subscribe to Steve Mould’s channel!

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:23.470
I would recommend his video on a self-pouring
liquid as being a good place to start.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:26.090
Next time, the tale of an engineering disaster.

